WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.900
Less than two months from the release of Avengers:
Endgame, we've still had no Hulk sightings.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:05.460
So where is he?

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.960
Until the movie arrives in theaters, there's
no way to know for sure, but for now, here's

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.150
a look at some of the most likely reasons
you haven't seen Hulk in the Avengers: Endgame

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:14.900
trailers.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.520
If the body count of Avengers: Endgame is
anything like what we saw in Avengers: Infinity

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.160
War, then there's good reason to assume the
Hulk might not survive the latest Avengers

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:25.660
adventure.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:30.320
In fact, you could argue that he has less
chance of surviving Endgame than most.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.180
We haven't heard a lot about whether Mark
Ruffalo plans to keep playing the character

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:39.340
after this movie, but we're pretty sure Marvel
won't be pursuing any solo Hulk films anytime

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:39.840
soon.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.380
Additionally, Endgame marks the end of the
"Hulk trilogy" that Ruffalo discussed with

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.440
MCU boss Kevin Feige that began with Thor:
Ragnarok.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.820
So if there's a time for the Hulk to take
a final bow, now seems pretty appropriate.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.380
If directors Joe and Anthony Russo were to
kill the Hulk, doing it early in the fight

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:02.180
might accomplish the same thing Hulk's beating
at the hands of Thanos did in Infinity War.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:06.700
Having Thanos defeat the Avengers' toughest
muscleman in the first few minutes of the

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.140
film let audiences know what kind of threat
the heroes were facing.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:15.280
The stakes are likely to be raised in Endgame,
and it may be that the filmmakers decided

00:01:15.289 --> 00:01:19.689
the only thing more effective than having
Thanos K.O. the Hulk in the first few minutes

00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:22.420
would be to have Thanos take Hulk out permanently.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:27.140
If that's the case, there might not actually
be much Endgame footage that wouldn't spoil

00:01:27.149 --> 00:01:30.679
the hero's death — hence his absence from
the trailers.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.099
But if we're being totally honest?

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.179
This doesn't seem too likely compared with
some of the other possible reasons we've come

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:36.680
up with.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.819
While green is the color most often associated
with the Hulk, over the years we've seen that

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.880
green and gamma rays don't always go hand
in hand.

00:01:45.169 --> 00:01:50.740
The Hulk was gray in his first 1962 comic
book appearance, and in the late '80s that

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.080
version of the Hulk made a successful return.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:59.609
In 2008 — the same year Marvel Studios released
the first two MCU films, Iron Man and Incredible

00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:04.919
Hulk — Marvel Comics introduced the world
to the Red Hulk, a more villainous Hulk with

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:05.980
bright red skin.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.400
So while it ain't easy being green, it may
be even harder for the Hulk to stay green.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.360
It's possible that the reason Marvel Studios
has held back footage of the Big Guy from

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.140
the Avengers: Endgame trailers is that they
plan to premiere one of these non-green Hulks

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.840
in Endgame and don't want to spoil the reveal.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.650
Of course, images snapped of the forthcoming
batch of Endgame action figures have already

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.440
revealed of a green-colored Hulk.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.400
So let's put this particular theory down as
similarly unlikely.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.880
In terms of the Hulk's appearance, Disney
was a little mean to us when they released

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.920
the Avengers: Infinity War trailers.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.900
Shots in the trailer clearly showed the Hulk
alongside Captain America, Bucky, the Falcon,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.580
and other heroes charging Thanos' army in
the Battle of Wakanda.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.420
That, of course, never actually happened.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.400
Sure, Bruce Banner got to take a pretty awesome
joyride in the Hulkbuster armor, but there

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.020
wasn't much genuine Hulk-smashing in Avengers:
Infinity War.

00:03:01.409 --> 00:03:05.780
It may be that the Hulk isn't in the Endgame
trailers because the Russos may be trying

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.700
to manage fan expectations.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.540
Who knows how much Hulk we'll get?

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.960
By keeping him out of the trailers, Marvel
Studios may be ensuring that this question

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:19.099
remains as unanswered as possible, so that
if he shows up at all, we'll all stand up

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.580
and cheer our faces off.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.660
Sort of like this:

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:28.900
“YESSS!"

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:35.560
One of the most beloved moments of 2012's
Avengers film was this classic line from Bruce

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:36.060
Banner:

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:36.940
"I'm always angry.”

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:48.920
It was a huge, crowd-pleasing moment and it
officially brought the team together after

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.020
two hours of bickering.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.280
While the green guy hadn't been in the movie
much before that moment, after that classic

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:55.700
line, he stole the show.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.180
There was this moment:

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:58.640
“And Hulk?

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:01.660
Smash."

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.280
And this moment:

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:19.140
And of course, the best moment of quite possibly
any movie ever made:

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.520
“I am a god, you dull creature!

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.800
And I will not be bullied by—"

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:32.500
And part of what made all these moments so
successful was the relative lack of Hulk in

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.460
the rest of the movie.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.320
Chances are good the Russos are building up
to a new version of the "I'm always angry"

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:38.820
line.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.360
Maybe they plan to redeem the Hulk's absence
and thrashing in Infinity War by giving the

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.040
green guy the same kind of memorable moment
he enjoyed in Avengers.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.840
In the meantime, maybe they're hoping the
Hulk's absence from the marketing will make

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:54.040
our hearts grow fonder — and our cheers
that much louder.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.240
"Hulk!

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:56.260
Hulk!

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.340
Hulk"

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:02.040
Of course, there's one more possible reason
we may not have seen the Hulk...and it's all

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.420
the Fresh Prince's fault.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:09.500
Not too long ago, Disney released footage
of Will Smith as Aladdin's blue-skinned genie.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:14.740
“You really don't know who I am… genie,
wishes, none of that ringing a bell?"

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.620
The online responses were...less than ideal.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:23.080
So could Marvel Studios, a subsidiary of Disney,
be holding the big green Hulk back because

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.120
they're still feeling burned after going blue?

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.220
"I feel like a f---ing idiot."

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.930
Could Marvel really, truly be worried about
their special effects in the biggest movie

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.440
they've ever released suddenly not looking
up to snuff?

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:35.180
"I'm kidding.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:35.800
Watch this."

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:36.600
Nah.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:41.080
If there's one thing we know for sure by now,
it's that Marvel Studios knows exactly when

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.400
to Hulk out, and how keeping the Hulk hidden
can actually make the biggest impact.

